  "title": "am, is, are — Positive statements and contractions",
  "learningObjective": "Choose am, is or are for a subject in a positive present statement, recognize common contractions, and complete one plural-subject example.",
  "learnerLevel": "A1",
  "hook": "I am excited. She is excited. They are excited. Why different verbs?",
  "script": "Listen to these three sentences.\nI am excited.\nShe is excited.\nThey are excited.\nWhy do we use three different words?\n\nAm, is, and are are forms of the verb be in the present.\nThe word we choose depends on the subject.\nWith I, we use am.\nWith he, she, and it, we use is.\nAnd with you, we, and they, we use are.\n\nIn everyday English, we often shorten these expressions.\nI am becomes I'm.\nShe is becomes she's.\nAnd they are becomes they're.\n\nNow, your turn!\nMy classmates ___ outside.\nIs it am, is, or are?\n[Pause for three seconds. Do not read the blank aloud.]\nThe answer is are!\nMy classmates are outside.\nBecause my classmates means they.\n\nRemember:\nI am.\nHe, she, it — is.\nYou, we, they — are.\nSee you in Part 2!",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "present-be",
      "explanation": "Am, is and are are present forms of be. In this lesson they describe a feeling or location."
    },
    {
      "id": "subject-agreement",
      "explanation": "Use am with I; is with he, she or it; are with you, we or they. A plural subject such as my classmates uses are."
    },
    {
      "id": "contractions",
      "explanation": "Common positive contractions include I am → I'm, she is → she's and they are → they're. The apostrophe marks omitted letters; the meaning stays the same here."
    }
  ],
